{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_4fzut4bc67jk4udkonofydrthivdtsuzhd3wlnbrr6sh74tsyf2a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The guard works correctly in both directions. Verification is complete.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation is correct — no changes to `src/lib.rs` were needed\n\nAll 54 differential tests pass. Every fix I made was to my own test harness, never to the translation.\n\n### Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports `driver` + `static_sum`; Rust exports exactly those two. Symbol diff **empty**; all undefined symbols resolve to libc/libgcc.\n- [x] **Phase B** — all 34 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (34 rows ↔ 34 tests, 0 unchecked).\n- [x] **Phase C** — all 17 `ERRORS.md` rows have a passing test (17 rows ↔ 17 tests), plus 3 generic FFI-boundary tests.\n- [x] **All feature combinations** — `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so default is the only combination. `check_features.sh` derives this mechanically from `Cargo.toml` rather than assuming it, and runs both `debug` and `release` (release matters: `panic = \"abort\"` + no overflow checks).\n\n### Things worth flagging\n\n**1. I mutation-tested the suite, because an all-green differential suite that can't fail proves nothing.** Five mutants injected into `lib.rs` — loop bound `10→9`, `wrapping_add→saturating_add`, product `+1`, `%d→%u`, initial `sum 0→1` — were each caught (27/37/37/37/54 failures). `lib.rs` was then restored and re-verified. (A `$TMPDIR` change between tool calls broke my restore mid-run and left all 5 mutations stacked in the file; I noticed and restored it explicitly.)\n\n**2. The overflow behavior needed testing beyond the prescribed build.** `sum += update` and `i * stride` are signed-overflow UB in C, and most error-surface rows sit exactly on those boundaries. The CMake build passes no `-O` flag, so agreeing with it would *not* have shown the Rust's `wrapping_*` choice is right — an optimizer has no reason to exploit UB at `-O0`. I added `check_optlevels.sh`, which builds the C out-of-tree at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` (never touching `c_src`) and reruns all 54 tests against each. All five agree.\n\n**3. Two harness hazards produced spurious failures and could equally have hidden real ones.** `driver` writes via C `printf`, so its output is on fd 1; capturing that is process-global. libtest's own `ok` got captured inside `driver`'s output, and Rust's `LineWriter` flushed a partial `test row32 ... ` line into the capture. Fixed by flushing both stdio layers and forcing serial execution — and I *enforce* serialization in `require_serial_execution()` rather than hoping for it, since a contaminated capture is a correctness risk, not just a flake. Verified the guard rejects `RUST_TEST_THREADS=4` and accepts `--test-threads=1`.\n\n**4. The `static int` accumulator required real care.** glibc dedups `dlopen` by `(st_dev, st_ino)`, so reloading the same path hands back an already-mutated accumulator — tests would have been order-dependent and mutually contaminating. The harness copies each `.so` to a unique temp path (distinct inode) per test to get a true `sum == 0`, then exercises carried-over state *deliberately* in rows 14–17 and 29–32.\n\nTwo caveats I did **not** paper over: the C's `static int` is unsynchronised, so the Rust's `UnsafeCell` + `unsafe impl Sync` faithfully reproduces a data race — correct, but not differential-testable, so no test asserts it. And `cargo test` alone does not emit the cdylib (the crate is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` only), so `cargo build` must precede it; the harness fails loudly rather than silently skipping. Both are recorded in `VERIFICATION.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":723,"cache_read_input_tokens":106131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":723,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95f246a7-fb23-4de5-b1b5-410f54559215","uuid":"ebc8cc18-f677-41e7-a35c-a734589ad5ec","timestamp":"2026-08-28T10:07:45.764Z"}
